ve  normalized that  and as mps we've normalized it  social media has changed the lives of  politicians they are more accessible to  constituents but easier targets too  politicians have reported receiving  increasing amounts of death threats and  abuse online in recent years especially  if they're women or from an ethnic  minority background a study found that  female ethnic minority mps received 41  percent of all abusive tweets aimed at  women parliamentarians despite there  being eight times more white women mps  in parliament some have spoken out about  the threats they receive especially  online i think that social media  companies need to do much more or to  ensure that people are safe online and i  think because people think they can do  it without  it being identified as then people feel  more emboldened to say things that they  wouldn't attribute to their name if  people knew it was them that were saying  it ultimately it is a balance between  protecting politicians and protecting  democracy and each one of the uk's 650  mps has a very different view over what  security they do and do not need
 

